  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nThe cold front has moved through the area and overnight cold air \nadvection has been left in its wake. This cold air advection will \ncontinue through the day today making out during the late \nmorning/early afternoon with northerly winds gusting to 30-35 mph. \nIn addition, a upper level trough will move through the area today \nushering in mostly cloudy to cloudy skies. This coupled with the \nstrong CAA will hold highs in the low to mid 50s. This evening a \nsurface ridge of high pressure will build into the area allowing \nwinds to relax as the upper level trough shifts east allowing skies \nto clear tonight. This will create a good radiational cooling setup \ninviting the potential for the first freeze of the season across the \nnorthwestern and northern CWA and a first frost for much of the rest \nof the CWA. While this is not expected to be a hard or killing \nfreeze with temperatures expected to fall into the low to mid 30s, \nany sensitive vegetation will need to be protected tonight. \nTomorrow, the surface ridge of high pressure will remain in control \nproviding for weak mixing. So, despite sunny skies, highs will hold \nin the upper 50s to lower 60s. Skies are expected to remain clear \ntonight night however, the surface ridge is expected to shift east \nof the area overnight. This will allow, light southerly winds to \ndevelop keeping temperatures from dropping below freezing however, \npatchy frost cannot be ruled out. Sunday, slight height rises \ncoupled with modest WAA and sunny skies will drive highs back to \nseasonal normals in the mid to upper 60s.\n\nThe extended period looks mostly quiet with broad upper level \nridging for the first half of the work week becoming more amplified \nover the central CONUS in the second half of the work week. This \nwill produce a warming trend through the week with temperatures on \nMonday starting the week slightly above normal with highs in the \nupper 60s to lower 70s. However, highs by Thursday and Friday will \nmove well above normal rising into the mid 70s to mid 80s. The next \nslight chance (15-25%) for precipitation doesn't arrive until Friday \nas a upper level trough move out from the southwestern CONUS into \nthe central/northern Plains weakening as it does.",
